.TH kill 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
kill \- send a signal to a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
[\fB\-\fIsignal\fR] \fIpid\fR ...
.br
.B kill \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
sends a signal to each process.  Without one it sends
.BR SIGTERM ,
which asks the process to finish; a process may catch that and tidy up,
or ignore it.
.SH THE SIGNAL
Either a number, as
.BR \-9 ,
or a full name
.I with
the
.B SIG
prefix and in capitals, as
.BR \-SIGKILL .
.PP
.B \-KILL
without the prefix is
.I not
accepted, and neither is
.BR \-sigkill :
both give
.RB `` "unknown signal" ''.
This is where most of the trouble with this command comes from.
.TP
.B \-l
List the names and numbers this
.B kill
knows, and exit.
.SH THE USEFUL SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP 1
Hang up.
.TP
.B SIGINT 2
Interrupt, as from the interrupt key.
.TP
.B SIGQUIT 3
Quit.
.TP
.B SIGKILL 9
Cannot be caught or ignored.  The last resort.
.TP
.B SIGTERM 15
Terminate, politely.  The default.
.TP
.B SIGCONT 18
Let a stopped process go on.
.TP
.B SIGSTOP 19
Stop the process.
.PP
Signal 0 sends nothing but still checks that the process exists, so
.B kill \-0
is the way to ask.
.SH NOTES
The shell has no
.B kill
built in, so this program is what runs, and it takes process ids only \-
not job numbers.
.PP
With no arguments at all it prints its usage and exits 0.
.PP
Killing process 1 does nothing useful:
.B init
should be told what to do with
.BR telinit (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR killall (1),
.BR telinit (1)
